# Architecture

## Purpose

This repository is organized for agent legibility first: the code, docs, and plans should make the system understandable and changeable by a fresh stateless agent with minimal guesswork.

## Code Root

Application code lives under `${APP_ROOT_DISPLAY}`.

## Domain-First Layout

Organize code by business domain first. Each domain should eventually follow this internal shape:

```text
${APP_ROOT_DISPLAY}/domains/<domain>/
  types/
  config/
  repo/
  service/
  runtime/
  ui/
  providers/
```

Cross-cutting concrete implementations live outside the domains, typically under:

```text
${APP_ROOT_DISPLAY}/infra/providers/
${APP_ROOT_DISPLAY}/app/wiring/
```

## Layer Responsibilities

- `types` - domain vocabulary, entities, value objects, result types, schemas
- `config` - domain-local policy, defaults, mappings, behavior knobs
- `repo` - persistence and external data access
- `service` - use cases and domain orchestration
- `runtime` - handlers, jobs, controllers, flow wiring
- `ui` - views, routes, presenters, components
- `providers` - explicit ingress for auth, telemetry, flags, queues, clocks, connectors

## Dependency Direction

Allowed direction:

`ui -> runtime -> service -> repo -> config -> types`

Additional rules:

- Cross-cutting infrastructure enters through `providers`.
- Cross-domain access should happen through explicit published interfaces, not deep internal imports.
- Framework, ORM, transport, and SDK types should not leak inward unless the boundary explicitly owns them.

## Bootstrap Status

This file is the initial contract only. As the project grows, encode the most important architectural invariants as lints or structural tests so agents can correct themselves mechanically.
